<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Stephen Decatur</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1779–1820</span></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Stephen Decatur was an officer of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states-navy">United States Navy</a>, famous for burning the captured frigate <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philadelphia">Philadelphia</a> in Tripoli harbour in 1804 during the First <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barbary-coast">Barbary</a> War. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-1812">War of 1812</a> he captured the British frigate Macedonian. In 1815 he led a squadron to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> in the Second Barbary War and forced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algiers">Algiers</a> to end tribute from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> and free American captives. He was killed in a duel in 1820.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/stephen-decatur.jpg" alt="Stephen Decatur" width="320" height="382" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Charles Bird King · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Charles_Bird_King_-_Stephen_Decatur_-_NPG.87.26_-_National_Portrait_Gallery.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
